const e='<figure>\n    <span class="device-frame"><svg class="device-frame-chrome" viewBox="0 0 320 200" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><rect x="0" y="0" width="320" height="184" rx="3" class="device-frame-bezel"/><path d="M146 184 L141 197 L179 197 L174 184 Z" class="device-frame-neck"/><rect x="118" y="196" width="84" height="4" rx="2" class="device-frame-base"/></svg><span class="device-frame-screen"><img src="/content/images/browser-fullscreen.webp" alt="A web page filling an entire external display corner to corner, with no browser interface and no black bars." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></span></span>\n    <figcaption>The same phone, the same cable, the same monitor. This is what the panel can do.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <h2>Cause one: mirroring is the wrong shape</h2>\n\n  <p>An iPhone screen is roughly 19.5:9, tall and narrow. A monitor or TV is 16:9. When iOS mirrors, it scales the phone\'s picture up as far as it will go while keeping its proportions, then pads whatever\'s left with black. On a 16:9 panel that costs you around 40% of the screen.</p>\n\n  <p>Standard apps can\'t opt out of this. Safari has no way to tell iOS "render this for the external display in landscape", so what goes to the monitor is always a copy of what the phone is drawing for itself. The bars come from the default behaviour, not from your hardware.</p>\n\n  <h2>Cause two: the display\'s own overscan</h2>\n\n  <p>The second cause is the screen rather than the phone. TVs in particular ship with overscan on, a leftover from analogue broadcast, which crops the edges of the incoming picture and pads it back out again. That shows up as a thin black frame all the way round even when the source is correct.</p>\n\n  <div class="note">\n    <p>If you see a <em>thin border on all four sides</em> rather than two thick bars, that\'s overscan. Look in the TV\'s picture menu for "Just Scan", "Screen Fit", "Full Pixel" or "1:1" and turn it on.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2>The fix: stop mirroring</h2>\n\n  <p><strong>XeOS: External Display Browser</strong> doesn\'t copy the phone\'s screen. It renders its own desktop directly for the external display, in landscape, at whatever resolution the panel reports, and switches overscan compensation off on its side so the image reaches the edges. The phone\'s screen becomes a trackpad and a controller while the monitor gets the environment.</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/desktop-home.webp" alt="A desktop environment filling a 16:9 external display edge to edge, with a dock along the bottom." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>Edge to edge, no letterboxing, at the display\'s own resolution.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <ol class="steps">\n    <li><strong>Connect the display</strong> with a USB-C to HDMI cable (iPhone 15 and later, all USB-C iPads), a Lightning Digital AV Adapter (iPhone 5 to 14), or AirPlay.</li>\n    <li><strong>Open XeOS with the display connected.</strong> It detects the screen and takes it over.</li>\n    <li><strong>Pick the resolution the panel reports</strong> in Settings › Display, usually 1920 × 1080. Choosing a mode the display natively understands is what removes the padding.</li>\n    <li><strong>Set the content scale</strong> so text is the right size for how far away you\'re sitting: 100% at a desk, 130–150% across a room.</li>\n    <li><strong>If a thin frame remains, fix the TV\'s picture size setting.</strong> That last one is on the display, not the phone.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <figure class="is-narrow">\n    <img src="/content/images/settings-display.webp" alt="The Display pane in XeOS Settings with a resolution list and a content-scale stepper." width="1600" height="1077" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>Settings › Display. Resolution decides the shape of the picture; content scale decides how big everything on it looks.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <h2>Going further than "no bars"</h2>\n\n  <p>Once the picture fills the panel, the green button on any window strips the interface off it as well, so the title bar, the tabs, the dock and the status overlay come away and the content occupies every pixel. That\'s how video and slides are meant to be watched.</p>\n\n  <figure class="is-inline">\n    <img src="/content/images/traffic-lights.webp" alt="Close-up of a window\'s traffic-light buttons with an extra grey button beside them." width="352" height="118" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>Green is full screen. Move the pointer to the top edge to bring the title bar back.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <p>And because it\'s a desktop rather than a mirror, you can use the screen you just reclaimed: windows side by side, a file manager, document viewers, a browser with an ad blocker on by default.</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/window-switcher.webp" alt="An app switcher overlay showing icons for several open windows on an external display." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>Removing the bars leaves room for more than one thing at a time.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <div class="cta">\n    <p><strong>XeOS: External Display Browser</strong> turns an iPhone or iPad into a full desktop on any monitor, TV or projector.</p>\n    <p><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/external-display-browser/id6758286241" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="app-store-link">Download XeOS on the App Store</a></p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class="faq">\n    <h2>Common questions</h2>\n\n    <h3>Do I need a different cable or a newer phone?</h3>\n    <p>No. Any iPhone or iPad that can output video at all is enough, and the cable you already have is fine. The bars were never a hardware limit.</p>\n\n    <h3>Does this work over AirPlay as well?</h3>\n    <p>Yes. Mirror to the Apple TV first, then go back to XeOS and it picks the display up and draws its own desktop to it. A cable is lower latency and charges at the same time.</p>\n\n    <h3>Why does one particular site still show bars?</h3>\n    <p>That\'s the site\'s own video player letterboxing content that isn\'t 16:9, which is correct behaviour. A 4:3 video genuinely has nothing to put in the corners. The app\'s own output has no bars.</p>\n\n    <h3>Will they come back after I unplug?</h3>\n    <p>No. The resolution choice is remembered per display, so the next time you connect the same screen you\'re straight back to a full picture.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>The whole connection process, with every screenshot, is in <a href="/guides/connect-iphone-ipad-to-monitor-or-tv">the setup guide</a>.</p>\n';export{e as default};
//# sourceMappingURL=iphone-external-display-no-black-bars-C0WEFEc1.js.map
